WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.670
- Let's close this one out,
with one last feel good story.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.208
This is What I'm Liking.

00:00:04.208 --> 00:00:06.420
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.180
I am always impressed
when artists can take

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.800
the most random pieces of trash

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.750
and turn it into eye-popping treasure.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.690
It's super cool where people find beauty.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.660
But we featured a few, such
artists, on the show before.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.820
We even paired one with a younger sculptor

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.440
for a giant project.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.950
Well recently, I came across
some art that's just as cool.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.420
It's all intricate depictions
of animals and sea creatures.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.060
We have this very creative
artist on the line right now.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.944
It's incredible.

00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:36.930
She's watching, NBC Connecticut at Three.

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.120
Everybody say hello, to Stephanie.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.020
Better known to her

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.284
thirty-thousand Insta
followers, as Sugar Fox.

00:00:43.284 --> 00:00:44.117
(crowd cheering)

00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:45.390
What's up?

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.410
- Hi!

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.243
- Oh, my God.

00:00:47.243 --> 00:00:48.690
First of all, your work is so beautiful.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.210
You're so talented, can you explain more

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.430
about what it's made of?

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.930
- Thank you so much.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.080
It's all recycled
materials, mostly plastics.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.343
Things like, Tupperware,
toys, extension cords.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.210
I keep it all basically in a
trash heap in my home studio.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.820
It's been donated to me in bits and pieces

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.170
over the years by friends,
family, members of my community.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.100
I've actually stopped taking donations.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.990
Because, it's become so overwhelming.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.610
It's just too much stuff.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.550
But, in terms of what I like
to make, it's usually animals.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.830
I mostly like doing animals as an artist

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.613
because of the personality and
the soul that forms in them.

00:01:25.613 --> 00:01:28.110
And the final step to that point,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.170
is just giving them a
name, I name everything.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.100
- I mean, it's so crazy.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.330
The seahorse, right now,
that I was just looking

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.920
at, it looks cool both ways.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.395
If you just left it without the paint.

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:38.490
It's so cool what you do.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.470
I just love that you see that.
- Thank you.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.480
- It's such an incredible gift.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.090
So, how did you get into sculpting trash?

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.850
Like, where did that, how did that happen?

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.110
- So, it's a really long story.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.680
But basically, in 2017, I had been working

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.340
as a sign artist for a grocery
store for about five years.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.980
I left that job very abruptly,
with nothing lined up.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.010
So, I figured I would try
to be a freelance artist.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.920
But with no income, I had
no money for supplies.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.440
So, I had been following some
trash artists on Instagram,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.240
and I just figured, you
know, trash is in surplus

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.490
and it's free, so it would be perfect

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.640
if I could figure it out, and
when I tried it, I loved it.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.430
The response was really
overwhelmingly nice,

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.140
and here we are five years later,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.900
and I'm really proud to
say that I am making it as

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.310
a freelance artist.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.820
- Oh that's incredible, way to go man.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.350
Take something you're amazing at,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.060
and you're passionate about
and then making something

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.820
beautiful for everybody,
it's a really cool gift man.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.160
Thank you so much Stephanie,
I'm liking your post right now,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.024
all right?

00:02:36.024 --> 00:02:36.870
(bell dinging)

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.220
- Thank you.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.050
- And that's not all,
our season long partner,

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.570
Pilot Pen, makers of Frixion
the go-to erasable pen

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.730
of "The Kelly Clarkson Show"
want to celebrate those

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.530
creating fearlessly, just you mam,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.569
so they're going to give you
$1,000 just for being you.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:51.497
(crowd cheering)

00:02:51.497 --> 00:02:53.373
- Oh thank you.

